# Tier 11 Implementation Plan — Embodied Network Shaping
## Planning the Graph, Not Just Over It: Geometry Value, Motion Envelopes, Reversibility Classes, and the Calibration Cost of Moving

**Status:** Engineering implementation plan. Tier 11 unfreezes assumption F3
(geometry and hardware are exogenous): relay placement and mobility, deployable
charger/beacon nodes, RIS orientation, antenna pointing, node sleep/spawn. The
kernel was proved without anyone calling it Tier 11: the jam-resistance
geometry constant Γ (D8 Thm. J1), the attrition placement rule (D8 Cor. J2.1,
eq. J.2), harvest-desert maps (D7 Thm. N1 pruning), and node RF values ν_w
(D1 eq. 14 / D7 N.5) are jointly the **geometry value function** this tier
optimizes. Foundations consumed: Tier 10 M5 (actuation-energy prices), the
Tier 8 Semantic Spec (the stack as a certified oracle per candidate geometry),
Tier 6 (the envelope pattern, lifted to actuators), O1-U3 (the two-timescale
convergence template).

**What Tier 11 is, in one sentence.** The slow layer that proposes changes to
the network's own geometry — where to put a charger, which relay to wake,
whether moving node 7 four meters buys more mission value than the joules it
costs — evaluated by the fast layers as an oracle, priced by Tier 10, executed
only inside pre-certified motion envelopes, with every irreversible act
ledgered as a committed resource.

**What it is not.** Not a robotics stack (physical drive/flight control is an
external system behind the ActuationGate; this plan owns the *decision* and
the authority boundary, and flags physical safety as out of scope and
externally owned). Not the full pursuit game against a mobile jammer
(deferred; §10). Not free: every move spends harvested or deployed energy and
*invalidates gain calibration* — §4.5 is the part of this plan most likely to
be underestimated.

