# 4. Algorithms

## 4.1 U1 — deploy-time placement (the standalone-value core)

Inputs: candidate site set, exogenous field forecast/scenarios, jammer-position
scenarios (CSR set), budgets (asset count, energy). Objective: weighted
combination declared per deployment of (i) desert elimination — cover every
mission-relevant node above `x_s` margin ν; (ii) Γ reduction; (iii) attrition
dominance in contested cells (place chargers strictly inside the jammer's
distance to each defended node — the closed-form J.2 criterion turns this into
a geometric covering problem); (iv) ν_w-weighted energy delivery.

Method, honestly tiered: greedy with local-search polish as the default
(coverage components are submodular-like; the latency component is not —
no global guarantee is claimed); exact MILP on small instances via the D3
exhaustive layer to measure the greedy gap; the gap is a certificate field
(the Test-7 discipline). **Robust variant:** min over placements of max over
the jammer-position scenario set — the inner max is |scenarios| cheap
evaluations; this is the shippable fragment of the position game (§10 defers
the rest).

## 4.2 U2 — runtime move generation

Three generators, by class: **R0 sets** — sleep/wake toggles scored by ΔV with
the woken node's energy state checked against I11.3; antenna/RIS gradient
steps. **R1 gradient moves** — for each mobile asset, the analytic ∂V/∂x
direction, step bounded by the I11.5 trust region and the envelope's
speed/energy caps; accepted iff `ΔV_certified ≥ price(joules) + margin`
(the beacon-activation rule pattern, D1 eq. 15, applied to motion).
**R2 proposals** — deployment suggestions from U1 re-run on the live field,
emitted as priced proposals only (I11.2). All candidates are scored by the
oracle (§4.3) before anything reaches the gate.

## 4.3 The oracle evaluation contract

A candidate geometry `g′` is scored by re-running the certified stack:
recompute fields at `g′` (gain updates), re-solve routing/scheduling (Ring-0/1
as configured), evaluate the Tier 8 objective, and *include the I11.4
penalty* — the value is computed under the **widened post-move bounds**, not
the pre-move calibrated ones. This ordering matters: a move that looks good
under calibrated gains and bad under widened ones is booked as bad until
re-calibration earns the difference back. ΔV is quoted as an interval
(Ring-1 gaps + calibration widening), Tier 10-style.

## 4.4 Two-timescale operation

Slow loop (geometry): U2 proposes at most one accepted change per dwell
window per asset. Fast loops (Tiers ≤10): converge per geometry as usual;
the O1-U3 template gives the convergence frame (fast layer contracts or is
causal-exact per its certificates; the slow layer changes parameters only at
fast-layer convergence). No new convergence proof is claimed beyond U3's
conditions; violations fall back to freeze-geometry (§7).

## 4.5 Post-move calibration (the underestimated cost)

Moving or deploying an asset invalidates every learned gain-operator entry
(PRISM P2′) involving it. The CalibrationManager: (i) at execution, flips the
touched Ĝ entries to free-space-plus-inflation with a conformal widening
recorded against the move; (ii) opens a re-calibration window in which the
prediction system's normal observation flow (packets-as-probes, ESU
integrals) re-estimates the entries; (iii) the earned-back width is credited
to the move's *realized* value in the Tier 10 quoted-vs-realized stream. Net
effect: the true cost of a move = joules + transient calibration debt, and
the system books both. Deployments into never-calibrated regions carry the
debt indefinitely until coverage exists — which U1's siting should weigh
(sites near existing calibrated paths are cheaper than their joules suggest).

