# 7. Failure modes and fallback chain

```
trigger                                  → response
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fast layer fails to re-converge at g′    → reject candidate; geometry frozen
realized ΔV misses quote (tracker)       → family revocation (Tier 10 U4
                                            pattern): that generator class
                                            stops proposing until re-validated
calibration debt exceeds budget          → no further moves touching the
                                            region until earned back
envelope/cert revocation mid-move        → driver-level safe-stop (external
                                            safety system authoritative);
                                            ledger the partial move as R1 debt
asset lost/stranded (R2 event)           → ReversibilityLedger commit;
                                            re-plan at the realized geometry
any of the above, persistent             → FREEZE GEOMETRY: Tier 11 off is
                                            exactly Tier ≤10 at current g —
                                            the network is never worse than
                                            its present physical configuration
```

# 8. Risk register

- **R1 — Calibration debt underestimated.** The classic failure: moves look
  good under stale calibrated gains. Mitigation: §4.3 ordering (score under
  widened bounds), §4.5 debt accounting, and prediction PR-style validation
  that post-move widening is honored. This is the risk this plan exists to
  surface; high likelihood of being the first real-world lesson.
- **R2 — Thrash.** Geometry oscillation under field drift. Mitigation: I11.6
  dwell + the Tier 10 hysteresis pattern + per-window move caps in envelopes.
- **R3 — Greedy placement gap.** No global guarantee on the mixed objective.
  Mitigation: MILP gap measurement on small instances (Test-7 discipline);
  the gap is reported, not hidden.
- **R4 — Energy double-spend.** Actuation drawing from the same harvested
  budget as control-plane and beacons. Mitigation: I11.3 hard margin checks +
  Tier 10 pricing makes the competition explicit.
- **R5 — Authority creep through envelope erosion.** Same shape as Tier 10
  R3; same mitigations, plus the absolute I11.2 line for R2 acts.
- **R6 — Physical safety.** Out of scope and externally owned, stated as
  such; the gate's safety predicates are necessary, not sufficient, and the
  plan claims nothing about the physical layer it does not control.

# 9. Promotion: Tests 1–7 plus T11-a/b/c

Inherited Tests 1–7 unchanged; additionally — **T11-a (geometry value):**
measured Γ reduction, desert-coverage gain, and mission-value gain per joule
of actuation, dominating the no-movement baseline at matched evidence
(quoted-vs-realized within tolerance, calibration debt included in the
realized side). **T11-b (adversarial):** under the jammer-position scenario
deck, robust placements realize their predicted worst-case value; mixed/
diversified placements dominate single-point placements where the scenarios
predict it. **T11-c (reversibility hygiene):** ledger audit clean — zero R2
acts without external authority, zero envelope violations, every committed
resource accounted — across the adversarial actuation deck.

Lifecycle: SHADOW → LIMITED-ACTIVE per reversibility class in order
(R0 → R1 → R2-proposals) → ACTIVE. Rollback drill: freeze-geometry from every
state, plus an R1 return-to-station drill within envelope energy caps.

# 10. What this plan deliberately defers

The full pursuit game against mobile jammers (D8 open #3): needs movement
models on both sides and the Tier 9 equilibrium machinery over geometry
mixtures; the shippable fragment (robust placement vs position scenarios) is
in U1/M5. Aerial/3-D relays and swarm coordination: actuator classes beyond
this plan's driver abstraction. Multi-gateway geometry (who moves whose
assets): blocked on Tier 7's authority boundary, same deferral logic as
Tier 9's coalition case. Joint geometry-and-schedule co-optimization in one
solver: the two-timescale split is deliberate — collapsing it re-opens the
hardness that the layering avoids, and nothing in T11-a requires it.

