## Initial Proposals

### Opus 4.8

## Ratified Council Consensus — Tier 11 "Embodied Network Shaping"

### 1. Strongest objection first

**The plan never demonstrates that the runtime actuation layer (R1/R2 moves) produces positive *net* mission value, and its own structure quietly concedes this.** Net value = certified ΔV − actuation joules − calibration debt − the compute/search cost of finding and trust-region-validating a beating move. The plan prices the first two terms well, foregrounds the third (§4.5), and **never prices the fourth at all.** Worse, it defines "failure" (§7, §8) as *invariant violation* — never as *"runs cleanly and never finds a move worth making."* That silent-uselessness outcome is the most probable real-world result for R1/R2, and it is invisible to the entire failure/risk apparatus.

Two tells confirm this is the load-bearing weakness, not a quibble:

- §6 states M1 (offline placement) "is the highest value-to-risk item in the entire tier and should ship even if runtime actuation never does." That is the plan admitting the bulk of its novel machinery (the gate, ledger, two-timescale loop, calibration manager — M2–M4) may not be worth building.
- The freeze-geometry floor ("Tier 11 off is exactly Tier ≤10 at current g") is presented as a safety net, but it is also the likely *equilibrium*: a large, complex system whose dominant behavior is to do nothing while consuming oracle compute.

So the §4.5 calibration cost — which the plan nominates as its biggest risk — is **the visible risk, not the decisive one.** It is the term the authors have a mitigation story for. The decisive risk is the *unpriced* combination of (search/oracle compute + calibration *time*) feeding an *unproven net-value case*, deferred to a post-build promotion test (T11-a) rather than gated before the runtime layer is written. Confidence: **moderate-high.**

### 2. What it is

Tier 11 unfreezes the assumption that geometry/hardware are fixed (F3). It is a **slow planning loop that proposes changes to the network's own physical configuration** — wake a relay, point an antenna, move a mobile node, deploy a charger — and decides whether each change buys more mission value than the energy it costs.

The core technical bet has four parts:

- **V(g) — geometry value function.** Not a new model: it is the *existing certified Tier ≤10 objective re-evaluated at a candidate geometry g*. The lower stack is used as an evaluation oracle. Cheap proxies (Γ jam-resistance via one Dijkstra, harvest-desert maps, ν_w RF values, attrition margins) guide search but do not certify.
- **R0/R1/R2 reversibility classes.** Phasing and authority are organized by reversibility, not capability: R0 reversible-cheap (sleep/wake, antenna, RIS), R1 reversible-costly (mobile repositioning), R2 committed/irreversible (physical deployment, stranding). R2 *always* requires external authority.
- **Motion envelopes + ActuationGate.** A direct lift of the Tier 6/Tier 10 authority pattern: pre-certified zones, speed/energy caps, machine-checkable predicates, expiry. The gate is the sole writer to geometry state; physical safety sits below it and is explicitly out of scope.
- **Two-timescale oracle loop.** Slow geometry layer changes parameters only at fast-layer (routing/control) convergence; ≤1 accepted move per asset per dwell window; inherits the O1-U3 convergence template with no new proof claimed.

Costs are priced through the Tier 10 energy ledger, and crucially, **every move invalidates the channel-gain calibration of the assets it touches** (§4.5) — the gains revert to widened free-space bounds until passively re-learned, and that "calibration debt" is charged against the move's realized value.

### 3. Soundness & novelty

**The central idea is coherent and non-trivial.** Unifying "where to place hardware" and "how to run the network" under one priced objective, with the certified stack as the value oracle, is a genuinely clean framing. Scoring candidates *under widened post-move bounds rather than stale calibrated ones* (§4.3) is the correct conservative discipline and is the intellectual spine of the document. Confidence: **high** that the framing is sound.

But the novelty is narrower than the "new tier" branding implies, and the plan is admirably honest about this ("the kernel was proved without anyone calling it Tier 11"):

- **Real new capability (two items only):** (a) closed-loop *runtime* geometry actuation with reversibility-graded authority, and (b) **calibration-debt-aware move pricing (§4.5)** — explicitly booking the loss of channel knowledge as a debt and crediting earned-back width to realized value. (b) is the one piece the broader literature usually gets wrong by assuming channel knowledge is free or instantly re-acquired; it is both the most novel idea and the biggest risk, which is internally consistent.
- **Mostly relabeling / disciplined reuse:** V(g) is "run the existing objective at different g" given a name. U1 PlacementOptimizer is classical robust facility-location / sensor-placement (greedy-submodular + small MILP + minimax robustness) — valuable, not novel, and the plan claims no global guarantee. MotionEnvelope/ActuationGate is *by the plan's own words* "a mirror of Tier 10 U3." The two-timescale loop "claims no new convergence proof." These are strengths for shippability and weaknesses for any novelty claim.

The uncomfortable intersection: the genuinely *new* capability is concentrated in exactly the runtime R1/R2 layer whose net value is unproven. The parts that are clearly valuable (M0/M1) are the parts that are *not* novel.

### 4. Feasibility as specified

- **§4.5 re-calibration cost — partially addressed, with an unbounded tail.** The mechanism (widen → re-cal window → credit earned-back) is sound. The unaddressed hard part is **re-calibration *time*, not cost.** If re-cal relies on passive "packets-as-probes," calibration speed depends on traffic through the moved asset — but a freshly moved/deployed asset gets little traffic until it is trusted on routes, and it won't be trusted until calibrated. Chicken-and-egg. The plan admits deployments into never-calibrated regions "carry the debt indefinitely" — and that worst case (deserts) is precisely where deployment value is highest. Confidence: **moderate-high** this is real.
- **§4.4 two-timescale convergence — the hidden crux.** U3 separation assumes the fast layer converges much faster than the slow layer perturbs. But calibration is a *slow* fast-layer process. After a move the fast layer converges quickly only to a *widened-bound* solution; re-tightening is slow and traffic-dependent. So either the slow loop waits for full re-cal before the next move (runtime actuation becomes useful only for very slow drift — minutes-to-hours per move) **or** it moves again before re-cal completes, violating the separation the entire architecture rests on. The plan does not resolve this. This binds §4.4 and §4.5 into one problem and is the deepest *technical* objection. Confidence: **moderate-high.**
- **§4.3 oracle contract — sound but compute-unbudgeted.** "Score under widened bounds" is right; interval ΔV is honest. But each candidate requires recompute-fields + re-solve-routing/scheduling + Tier 8 eval, and **the plan never bounds how many oracle calls per dwell** nor validates "Ring-0 cheap." Gradient guidance helps continuous R1 but R0 sleep/wake *sets* are combinatorial with no gradient. This is the unpriced fourth term from §1.
- **§1.3 invariants — the strongest part of the document.** I11.3 (actuation charged against StabilityCertificate margins, "infeasible by construction") and I11.2 (R2 always external authority) are excellent. One soft spot: I11.3 guarantees only against *planned* energy; an R1 move whose return-energy was underestimated (wind, terrain) can still strand an asset, silently converting an R1 into an unplanned R2. The §7 fallback catches it but the "by construction" guarantee is softer than stated.
- **§6 staging — well-sequenced.** Observability → offline tool → R0 shadow → R1 → R2-proposals, lowest-reversibility-first, SHADOW before LIMITED-ACTIVE, is correct discipline. M3 (R1 + calibration debt live) is where the §4.4/§4.5 timescale problem will blow the 3–4 week estimate.
- **§7 fallback — adequate for *safety*, blind to *uselessness*.** Freeze-geometry-equals-status-quo is a genuinely strong floor, and family-revocation is a good closed loop. But every trigger is a *violation*; none is "no move beat baseline this window." The most likely outcome is not in the table.
- **§8 risk register — competent on what it lists, blind exactly where the framing points away.** It spends its honesty budget on R1/calibration-debt (which *has* a mitigation) and omits: (i) oracle/search compute budget, (ii) calibration-time-vs-dwell separability, (iii) net-value / silent-uselessness. Those three are the ones without easy mitigations — which is plausibly why they're absent.

### 5. Recommendation

**BUILD WITH NAMED MODIFICATIONS.**

Do not KILL — M0/M1 are low-risk, genuinely useful, and the safety floor is solid. Do not BUILD AS SPECIFIED — the runtime layer's net value is unproven and its compute/timescale risks are unpriced. Do not DEFER the whole tier — that wrongly buries the good offline pieces.

**Single highest-value change: insert a hard net-value gate between M1 and M2.** Before any runtime actuation code is written, require a SHADOW-mode empirical demonstration on real field traces that the move generators find moves whose certified ΔV (scored under widened post-move bounds per §4.3) exceeds **joules + measured calibration debt + a charged search/compute term**, dominating the freeze-geometry baseline — *with the re-calibration clock running at its true passive rate.* If R0 clears it but R1/R2 cannot, ship M0/M1, wire R0, and stop.

This is decision-forcing because it converts the plan's own implicit admission ("M1 should ship even if runtime never does") into an explicit go/no-go, and because it catches all three unregistered risks at once: a too-long calibration time, an unaffordable search budget, or a thin value signal will all show up as negative net value in shadow, *before* the expensive machinery exists. T11-a already names this comparison — but as a *promotion test after building*, which is exactly the wrong place for it. Move it earlier and make it a gate.

**What would change the verdict toward BUILD AS SPECIFIED (for R0/R1):**
- Evidence the oracle re-solve is genuinely sub-second warm-started *and* field drift is slow (dwell windows of minutes-to-hours), making the compute term negligible.
- An *active* re-calibration path (inject probe traffic to re-cal a moved asset in seconds rather than passively waiting for organic traffic) — this dissolves the §4.4/§4.5 timescale tension that is the deepest technical risk.
- An existing shadow trace already showing positive net value — in which case the gate is passed and you build.

Confidence in the overall recommendation: **moderate-high.** The plan is unusually well-engineered on safety and honesty; the gap is economic, and it is located precisely where the document's framing directs attention away from it.

### ChatGPT 5.5

**1. Strongest Objection First**

The plan’s biggest failure mode is that it treats post-move calibration debt as “accounted for” without specifying a hard calibration contract: how wide the post-move bounds become, how fast they shrink, what observations are required, and when a move is disallowed because uncertainty, not joules, dominates.

That is not a minor missing detail. §4.5 says every move invalidates gain entries and deployments into uncalibrated regions can carry debt indefinitely. If that debt is large or slow to retire, then the oracle in §4.3 may reject nearly all useful moves, or worse, accept moves whose realized value cannot be validated quickly enough. This directly threatens R1 mobility, R2 deployment proposals, the two-timescale assumption, and the claimed 6-7 week path to live actuation.

**2. What It Is**

Tier 11 is a slow decision layer that changes the physical/network geometry itself: sleep or wake nodes, point antennas/RIS, move relays, suggest charger/beacon/relay deployments, and price those actions against energy and commitment cost.

Core bet:

- Define a geometry value function `V(g)` by running the certified lower-tier stack at candidate geometry `g`.
- Use cheap geometry signals, Γ, harvest deserts, ν_w, attrition margins, and gradients, to generate candidate moves.
- Classify actions by reversibility:
  - `R0`: cheap reversible changes.
  - `R1`: costly reversible movement.
  - `R2`: committed/irreversible deployment or abandonment.
- Execute only through `MotionEnvelope` plus `ActuationGate`.
- Use a two-timescale loop: fast lower tiers converge at each geometry; slow Tier 11 changes geometry only after convergence.
- Charge both actuation energy and calibration debt, because moving changes the gain model.

**3. Soundness & Novelty**

The central idea is coherent and non-trivial: plan over the network’s own physical geometry, not merely routing over a fixed graph, while using lower tiers as an evaluation oracle and Tier 10-style pricing/ledgering for costs.

Real capability added:

- Geometry becomes a controllable state variable.
- Reversibility classes create a useful authority boundary.
- Motion envelopes adapt an existing safety/authority pattern to physical actuation.
- Calibration invalidation is correctly recognized as a first-class cost.
- The oracle loop avoids pretending the cheap gradient/objective proxies are sufficient for certification.

Where it is mostly renaming existing machinery:

- ActuationGate is explicitly a mirror of Tier 10 U3.
- Hysteresis, family revocation, quoted-vs-realized tracking, and trust regions are inherited patterns.
- The two-timescale convergence story is not new proof; §4.4 admits it only reuses the O1-U3 frame.
- Much of `V(g)` is assembled from existing Γ/desert/ν/attrition components.

So: novelty is not in the math primitives individually. It is in making geometry mutable under certified evaluation, authority gating, energy pricing, and calibration debt.

**4. Feasibility As Specified**

The hard parts are real, and the plan identifies the right ones, but underspecifies the decisive one.

- §4.5 calibration: correctly flagged, but not operational enough. Missing: uncertainty budgets, sample/observation requirements, timeout behavior, debt pricing formula, and promotion gates proving debt retires fast enough.
- §4.3 oracle contract: directionally sound. Scoring under widened bounds is the right ordering. But the contract is incomplete unless the widened-bound construction is quantified and consistently comparable across candidates.
- §4.4 two-timescale loop: acceptable only if geometry changes are rare and fast-layer convergence is actually observed before each move. The fallback is plausible, but no bound is given for how often candidates fail or how expensive repeated oracle evaluation is.
- §1.3 invariants: strong on authority, energy margin, envelopes, calibration invalidation, trust regions, and hysteresis. They are necessary, not sufficient.
- §6 milestones: M0/M1/M2 are credible. M3 is where risk concentrates. R1 mobile moves should not enter live actuation until calibration debt behavior has been measured in SHADOW.
- §7 fallback chain: useful, but slightly overclaims. “Tier 11 off is exactly Tier ≤10 at current g” is true only after the system accepts the realized physical geometry and any calibration widening already caused by partial moves.
- §8 risk register: honest, but it names calibration debt without turning it into a kill/pass gate.

**5. Recommendation**

**BUILD WITH NAMED MODIFICATIONS.**

Single highest-value change: add a mandatory **CalibrationDebtContract** before M3.

It should define, for every touched gain class: widened-bound formula, debt budget, required observation/probe volume, expected retirement window, acceptance threshold, timeout/freeze behavior, and quoted-vs-realized promotion criteria. Make R1 live movement blocked until SHADOW runs show calibration debt is bounded and retires within the dwell/control timescale.

Verdict confidence: **moderate-high**. The architecture is sound enough to build M0-M2 and M1 has standalone value. The plan should not proceed to R1/R2 as written without making calibration debt a quantified gate rather than an acknowledged risk.

### Kimi K2.6

**Council Framing Note.** The prompt treats this document as a buildable engineering implementation plan with a 6–7 week path to live actuation. The strongest alternative framing is that Tier 11 is a **research validation problem** whose central hypothesis—online physical actuation can be certified by an oracle that the very act of moving invalidates—has not yet been proven feasible on the target platform. Under the engineering-framing, the staging in §6 looks reasonable; under the research-framing, the plan attempts to construct an actuation pipeline before validating its core physical-to-cyber feedback loop. That broader question changes the recommendation materially (see §4 and §5).

---

### 1. STRONGEST OBJECTION FIRST

The single most serious reason this plan could fail is a **structural epistemic mismatch between the optimizer’s guidance and the oracle’s certification**: the runtime move generator (§4.2) is guided by sharp, closed-form geometric gradients and cheap heuristic components—Γ(g), ν_w fields, attrition margins, ∂T/∂x_w (§2, §4.2)—but the ActuationGate is required to certify every candidate under **deliberately degraded, widened radio bounds** that erase the precision those gradients assume (I11.4, §4.3). The text acknowledges that “planning that ignores this books value it has not yet earned,” yet it provides no proof, measurement, or even argument that gradient proposals are correlated with certified value under widened bounds; it only offers “trust regions” (I11.5) that bound position, not the calibration-degradation itself.

Compounding this, the calibration debt is **open-ended and unbounded**: R2 deployments into “never-calibrated regions carry the debt indefinitely until coverage exists” (§4.5). The system therefore risks either (a) **paralysis**, where every gradient-suggested move fails the pessimistic oracle and Tier 11 does nothing useful, or (b) **irreversible commitment**, where R2 moves pass the gate before their debt is understood and permanently strand resources in uncalibrated, possibly zero-value geometry. The §4.5 “Accounting” treats this as a ledger entry; in an energy-constrained, contested environment it is a **hard feasibility kill-switch** that the plan has not tamed.

**Confidence: High.** This objection is grounded directly in the text’s own statements about widened bounds, indefinite R2 debt, and §4.5 being the most underestimated section.

---

### 2. WHAT IT IS

In plain language, Tier 11 is a **slow supervisory loop that asks “what if we physically reconfigured the network?” and answers by treating the existing certified network stack as a simulator/oracle.** It proposes geometry changes—move a mobile relay four meters, wake a sleeping node, deploy a charger, repoint an antenna—and scores each proposal by running the full Tier ≤10 routing, scheduling, and objective evaluation at the candidate geometry before any hardware moves. Executed changes are wrapped in pre-certified safety zones (MotionEnvelopes) and reversible-cost ledgers, and they are priced in joules and shadow values via Tier 10.

**The core technical bets are fourfold:**
- **V(g), the geometry value function:** The mission value of a physical layout *g* is defined as the objective the lower tiers already certify (Tier 8), now evaluated at that layout. The plan notes that several quantities (jam-resistance Γ, harvest-desert maps, node RF values ν_w, attrition margins) already existed and are bundled to guide search (§2).
- **Reversibility classes R0/R1/R2:** A taxonomy of how easily an act can be undone. R0 (sleep/wake, antenna pointing) is cheaply reversible; R1 (relay repositioning) is reversible but energetically costly; R2 (deployment, battery-irreversible spend) is permanently committed (§1.1).
- **MotionEnvelopes + ActuationGate:** Pre-certified physical zones and authority boundaries (lifted from Tier 6) that restrict *where* and *how fast* hardware can move, and which enforce that R2 acts can never auto-execute (I11.1, I11.2, §5).
- **Two-timescale oracle loop:** The fast layer (Tiers ≤10) converges routing and scheduling at a fixed geometry and acts as the oracle; the slow layer (Tier 11) changes the geometry only when the fast layer has converged, using the O1-U3 template (§4.4).

**Confidence: High.** This summary is a direct reading of §1, §2, §4, and the component diagram in §3.

---

### 3. SOUNDNESS & NOVELTY

**Is the central idea coherent?** Yes, as an **architectural pattern**: outer-loop physical optimization with inner-loop network certification is logically coherent and the tiered reversibility model is a sound safety abstraction. However, its coherence as a *closed loop* is **conditional and unproven** in the text. The plan explicitly disclaims any new convergence proof for the two-timescale loop under geometry changes (§4.4), and it admits the calibration mechanism is speculative (§4.5).

**Where is it merely renaming existing machinery versus adding real capability?**
- **Largely renaming / integrative:** The “geometry value function” V(g) is a portfolio of pre-existing D1/D7/D8 quantities (Γ, harvest-desert, ν_w, attrition rule) assembled under a new name (§2). The two-timescale template is O1-U3; the oracle is the Tier ≤10 stack; the envelope pattern is Tier 6. Tier 11 consumes these without altering their internal logic.
- **Real additions:** (i) The **ReversibilityLedger** and the hard external-authority requirement for R2 (I11.2), which is a genuine policy/authority innovation; (ii) the **runtime gradient-based move generator** with trust regions (§4.2, I11.5), which packages existing closed-form derivatives into a live proposal loop; and (iii) the **calibration debt accounting framework** (§4.5), which is conceptually novel to this tier even if it is admitted to be brittle.

**Non-triviality:** The tier is non-trivial as an integration exercise, but the text itself undercuts claims of deep novelty by noting that “the kernel was proved without anyone calling it Tier 11” and that the components in §2 are existing theorems/equations. The actual scientific risk lies in the **online closure** of the calibration loop, which the text treats as accounting rather than as an open research question.

**Confidence: Moderate.** The architectural concept is sound; the online instantiation is speculative.

---

### 4. FEASIBILITY AS SPECIFIED

**§4.5 Post-move gain re-calibration cost — LOW confidence.**  
The text itself flags this as “most likely to be underestimated.” The mechanism relies on “the prediction system’s normal observation flow (packets-as-probes, ESU integrals)” to recover invalidated gain entries, but it offers **no bound on convergence time or packet budget**, and it admits that some deployments carry the debt “indefinitely.” In an energy-constrained, possibly jammed environment (the implied context of D8), “normal observation flow” is not a given. Without a hard cap on outstanding widened entries, the system can enter a mode where the oracle is permanently degraded and the network is operating blind.

**§4.3 Oracle evaluation contract — MODERATE confidence.**  
Logically sound: scoring under widened bounds is the safe ordering. However, the text asserts that re-solves are “warm-started, Ring-0 cheap” but provides **zero operational evidence** (no flop count, no latency, no energy-per-query). If evaluating a single candidate consumes non-negligible energy relative to the actuation energy saved, the oracle loop violates the program’s energy-constrained premise. The plan omits this risk entirely.

**§4.4 Two-timescale convergence — LOW-TO-MODERATE confidence.**  
The plan states, “No new convergence proof is claimed beyond U3's conditions; violations fall back to freeze-geometry.” This is honest but weak: geometry changes that invalidate calibration are structurally different from the parameter perturbations O1-U3 likely addressed. The fallback is safe (the network “is never worse than its present physical configuration”), but it means Tier 11 may spend most of its operational life frozen, providing little incremental value for the engineering cost.

**§1.3 Invariants — MODERATE confidence.**  
I11.1–I11.6 are necessary hygiene. Their weakness is **omission**: there is no global invariant bounding total calibration debt, no invariant guaranteeing closure of the re-calibration window, and no invariant limiting oracle-query compute cost. I11.4 states the degradation without arresting it.

**Staging / Milestones (§6) — MODERATE confidence for M0/M1; LOW confidence for M3 onward.**  
M0 (observability dashboards) and M1 (offline placement tool) are low-risk, standalone deliverables; the text correctly identifies M1 as “the highest value-to-risk item in the entire tier.” However, the critical path to M2/M3 claims live R0 then R1 actuation within roughly **6–7 weeks**, with no experimental gate between reversible-cheap (R0) and reversible-costly (R1) actuation that validates whether the widened-bound oracle actually discriminates among candidates or whether calibration recovers on target hardware. That timeline conflates software integration with physical phenomenology.

**Failure-mode fallback chain (§7) — MODERATE confidence.**  
The chain is logically complete for point failures (convergence fail → freeze; tracker miss → revoke; envelope breach → safe-stop). It is **silent on systemic cascading calibration collapse**: a sequence of R1 moves in a small region could mutually invalidate one another’s links faster than the CalibrationManager can close windows, yet the fallback chain only reacts per-move. “Re-plan at the realized geometry” assumes the oracle remains usable, which may not be true if the geometry is largely uncalibrated.

**Risk register (§8) — MODERATE confidence.**  
R1 is honestly included but mitigated only with “accounting,” not a hard kill switch. The register omits two risks visible in the text but unlisted: **(i)** Oracle-query computation/energy cost (the energy-constrained stack evaluating many candidates may spend its own battery), and **(ii)** Signal destruction under widened bounds (if the Tier ≤10 objective interval collapses to indistinguishability under I11.4 widening, the gate cannot function).

---

### 5. RECOMMENDATION

**BUILD WITH NAMED MODIFICATIONS.**

**Single highest-value change:** Insert a **Calibration-Closure Gate** as a hard, blocking milestone between M2 (R0) and M3 (R1). This gate must require **empirical, on-target demonstration**—using the actual hardware, packet-as-probe mechanism, and operational RF environment—that every reversibility class can recover calibrated gain entries to a stated precision within a **finite, pre-specified budget of time, energy, and packets**. Until this gate is passed, Tier 11 must be restricted to:
- M0/M1 (observability and offline placement, which the text correctly identifies as high standalone value); and
- M2 R0 actuation **only in SHADOW mode** (ledgered, not executed, per the plan’s own safe staging).

Coupled with this gate, add an **Invariant I11.7 (Calibration Debt Cap):** a hard network-wide limit on the number/fraction of widened gain entries permitted at any time; if the cap is reached, the system automatically freezes all geometry changes and halts R1/R2 proposals until the CalibrationManager reports closure.

**Why this changes the verdict:** The plan as written treats R1/R2 runtime actuation as an incremental engineering step (M2→M3→M4). The modification forces the plan to acknowledge that **online embodied shaping is a hypothesis, not a subsystem**, and demands experimental proof of the hypothesis before irreversible or energetically costly hardware is committed. If the gate fails, Tier 11 still ships as a valuable offline planning and observability layer (M1), which is a concrete win with zero runtime risk. If the gate passes, the remaining R1/R2 build-out proceeds from validated foundations.

**What would change my mind:** Empirical data showing that, under worst-case field conditions (low packet rate, contested spectrum), the oracle under I11.4 widened bounds maintains discriminative power—that is, it reliably ranks candidate geometries in the same order as the true post-calibration value—and that the CalibrationManager consistently closes re-calibration windows within the energy and time budgets of the mobile assets. Absent such data, online R1/R2 actuation is speculation funded by hardware.

### DeepSeek V4 Pro

### 1. STRONGEST OBJECTION FIRST

The single most serious reason this plan could fail as written is the **calibration-debt death spiral**. Moving any asset invalidates the gain-operator entries for every link that includes it, forcing the oracle to evaluate candidate geometries under free-space-plus-inflation widened bounds (§4.5). If those widened bounds are significantly worse than the calibrated gains, the oracle will systematically reject moves as not value-positive, because the post-move value assessed by the widened model looks worse than the pre-move value under calibrated gains. The plan relies on a “re-calibration window” where normal packet-probe traffic re-estimates the gains and credits back the “earned-back width.” However, there is no guarantee that this re-calibration can happen quickly, or at all: a moved asset may create new links with no existing traffic, and deployments into “never-calibrated regions” carry the debt indefinitely. The system then freezes geometry — Tier 11 is off, exactly the fallback — and the actuation capability adds zero value, permanently. The plan acknowledges the calibration cost as “most underestimated” (§4.5, Risk R1) but provides no concrete bound on the debt’s magnitude, no timeout that forces acceptance of the widened bounds, and no proof that the prediction system can re-learn channel gains within a useful timescale. This is not a peripheral risk; it is the core failure mode that can silently turn the whole tier into a no-op, and the plan’s mitigations (scoring under widened bounds, debt tracking) do nothing to break the resulting deadlock.

### 2. WHAT IT IS

Tier 11 is a slow control layer that decides when and where to physically reshape the network — moving relays, waking/sleeping nodes, deploying chargers or beacons, adjusting antennas or RIS states — to improve mission performance (jam resistance, coverage, energy delivery) while staying within harvested-energy budgets. It does *not* control the robots or physical motors; instead, it issues actuation commands that are executed only inside pre‑certified safety envelopes. The **core technical bet** is that the existing certified stack (Tiers ≤10) can act as a value oracle for any candidate geometry, and that by pricing the energy cost of moving via Tier 10’s ledger and explicitly accounting for the temporary loss of gain‑calibration accuracy, the system can safely and autonomously find geometry improvements without solving a monolithic joint optimization of topology and routing.

### 3. SOUNDNESS & NOVELTY

The central idea is **coherent** and **non‑trivial**. It properly decouples the slow geometry search from the fast routing/scheduling layers, reuses the lower-tier stack to certify ΔV, and enforces safety via motion envelopes inherited from Tier 6. The architecture is largely a disciplined composition of existing patterns (two‑timescale template O1‑U3, oracle‑evaluation pattern from Tier 10/U1, envelope pattern from Tier 6).

- **Real capability added:**  
  (a) The explicit geometry value function V(g) built from already‑proven quantities (Γ, attrition rule, harvest‑desert maps, ν_w field) turns theoretical theorems into a directly optimizable objective.  
  (b) The reversibility‑class ledger (R0/R1/R2) with external‑authority gating for irreversible acts is a genuine new control concept.  
  (c) **Treating gain‑calibration invalidation as a transient cost that is booked, tracked, and must be earned back** (§4.5) is a novel and valuable idea — it makes an otherwise hidden fragility of mobile networks visible and accountable in the planning loop.  

- **Merely renaming existing machinery:**  
  The two‑timescale template, the U3 pattern for the ActuationGate, and the oracle‑evaluation contract are direct lifts; the “geometry gradient” from existing closed‑form derivatives is existing knowledge. These parts do not add new science, but they are applied correctly.

### 4. FEASIBILITY AS SPECIFIED

The hardest part is unquestionably the **post-move calibration re‑cost** (§4.5). The feasibility hinges on whether the re‑calibration window can shrink fast enough. The plan states that “packets‑as‑probes, ESU integrals” will re‑estimate gains, but it offers no timing model, no lower bound on re‑convergence speed, and no mechanism to force traffic onto newly formed links. The siting guideline (“sites near existing calibrated paths are cheaper”) is soft advice, not a hard constraint, so U1 could still propose moves that create persistent debt. If the debt never clears, the system is stuck in the fallback — geometry frozen — forever.  

The **oracle evaluation contract** (§4.3) requires re‑running the certified stack per candidate. With warm‑started Ring‑0/1 solvers and a limited number of candidates per dwell window, this is likely computationally feasible, but the plan does not quantify the candidate budget or the worst‑case evaluation time, which is a minor omission.  

The **two-timescale convergence** (§4.4) inherits the conditions of the O1‑U3 template; the plan openly states that no new proof is claimed and that violations fall back to freeze‑geometry. This is safe but means Tier 11 can be disabled by fast‑layer divergence, which is exactly what widened post‑move bounds might cause.  

The **invariants** (§1.3) are well‑defined and enforceable within the architecture: MotionEnvelope enforcement, external authority for R2, margin checks in I11.3, gain‑invalidation in I11.4, trust regions in I11.5, and hysteresis in I11.6. They collectively prevent uncontrolled damage.  

The **staging/milestones** (§6) are realistic and deliver standalone value early (M0 observability, M1 offline placement tool). The critical path M0→M2 in ~6–7 weeks is plausible. The **failure‑mode fallback chain** (§7) is complete and guarantees that the network is never worse than its current physical configuration; freeze‑geometry is well‑integrated. The **risk register** (§8) correctly identifies the calibration debt as R1 and “most underestimated,” but it does not admit that the only mitigation — “no further moves touching the region until earned back” — can be permanent. The register therefore hides the true stochastic‑death risk: small moves can cause indefinite paralysis.

### 5. RECOMMENDATION

**BUILD WITH NAMED MODIFICATIONS.**

The single highest‑value change: **Add a calibration‑debt cap and permanent‑bound fallback mechanism.**  

Concretely, modify §4.5 and the CalibrationManager to enforce a per‑link or per‑region *maximum re‑calibration window* (time or number of probe opportunities). Once that window expires without earning back the widened width, the system must *permanently adopt the widened (or a conservatively widened) gain model as the new calibration baseline* for that link. This breaks the deadlock: the move’s real cost then includes that permanent degradation, and the oracle evaluates future moves under those fixed conservative gains. The system can then choose to accept that some channels are permanently worse than free‑space and plan accordingly, rather than stalling forever.  

This modification directly addresses the strongest objection without sacrificing the essential novelty of the calibration‑debt accounting. It turns an indefinite liability into a bounded, decision‑forcing cost. The offline placement tool (M1) should be shipped regardless, as it carries zero runtime risk and provides immediate value. The runtime actuation (M2–M5) should be gated on a proven demonstration that the re‑calibration loop can converge within the chosen cap under realistic traffic and probe densities. If that demonstration fails, M1 still stands; if it succeeds, Tier 11’s full promise is unlocked.

## Deliberation Round 1

### Opus 4.8

## Critique of the four proposals

All four converge on the verdict (**BUILD WITH NAMED MODIFICATIONS**), on the diagnosis that §4.5 calibration debt is load-bearing, on shipping M0/M1 regardless, and on the freeze-geometry floor being a genuine strength. The disagreement is narrow and, on inspection, reconcilable: *which single modification is primary*.

**Where the three calibration-first proposals (ChatGPT, Kimi, DeepSeek) are right and I adopt them:**
- ChatGPT's **CalibrationDebtContract** (widened-bound formula, debt budget, observation/probe volume, retirement window, acceptance threshold, timeout behavior) is the correct *instrument*. Without it the debt term is unmeasurable, and an unmeasurable term cannot be gated. Adopt.
- DeepSeek's **timeout→permanent-baseline** is the only proposal that *mechanically breaks the deadlock* the strongest objection names. Gating on calibration closure (Kimi) or contracting it (ChatGPT) detects the indefinite-debt trap but does not escape it; DeepSeek's "on window expiry, adopt the conservatively-widened gains as the new baseline and re-price future moves against them" converts an indefinite liability into a bounded cost. Adopt.
- Kimi's **research-vs-engineering framing** and **I11.7 global debt-cap → auto-freeze** are both correct. The debt-cap (global) and DeepSeek's permanent-baseline (per-link) are complementary at different scopes, not competing. Adopt both, layered.

**Where my proposal is the superset, and the calibration gates nest under it:** a calibration-closure gate is *necessary but not sufficient*. Even with instant re-calibration, the runtime layer earns its keep only if value-positive moves exist *and can be found within an affordable oracle/search budget*. The plan prices joules and (partially) calibration, foregrounds §4.5, but **never prices the oracle/search compute term** (§4.3 asserts "Ring-0 cheap" with no candidates-per-dwell bound; in an energy-constrained stack, oracle evaluation itself spends battery — Kimi's omitted-risk (i)/(ii)), and **defines failure only as invariant violation, never as "ran clean, found nothing worth doing."** That silent-uselessness outcome is the most probable R1/R2 result and is invisible to §7 and §8. The net-value gate is the single criterion that catches all three unregistered risks (slow calibration, unaffordable search, thin value signal) at once. So the correct top-level move is a **net-value gate**, *operationalized through* the CalibrationDebtContract — they are instrument and criterion, not alternatives.

**One technical point all four underweight:** §4.4 and §4.5 are coupled. Calibration is a *slow* fast-layer process, so "fast-layer convergence to tightened bounds" is slow and traffic-dependent. The slow loop then either waits for re-cal (runtime actuation is useful only for very slow drift) or moves before re-cal (violating the two-timescale separation the architecture rests on). The clean dissolver is an **active re-calibration path** (inject directed probe traffic, priced) instead of passive packets-as-probes. DeepSeek gestures at this ("no mechanism to force traffic onto newly-formed links"); I make it an explicit named modification.

**Net:** the proposals are compatible. The maximal candidate is net-value-gate-as-umbrella + CalibrationDebtContract-as-instrument + timeout→permanent-baseline and global debt-cap as deadlock-breakers + active probing as the timescale fix. Remaining open point for round 2: whether peers accept subordinating their calibration gates under the net-value umbrella, or insist calibration-closure is the standalone primary gate.

